    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1905, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Emperor Gojong</a> to abdicate in 1907 and annexed the country by the Japan–Korea Treaty of 1910, ruling it through a Governor-General of Chōsen based in Keijō.</li><li>Under the theory of Nissen dōsoron the colonial government pursued Japanization, closing Korean newspapers, phasing out all Korean language courses by 1943 and pressing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> to take Japanese surnames under the Sōshi-kaimei decrees of 1939.</li><li>From 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> mobilised around 5.4 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> for its war effort; many were moved by force to mines and factories, and women and girls were forced into sexual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> as &quot;comfort women&quot; for Japanese soldiers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The colonial authorities destroyed 85 percent of all the buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a> Palace, and built the Japanese General Government Building on the exact site of its primary building.</p></aside>
    </section>

