    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> open with the unequal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ganghwa">Japan–Korea Treaty of 1876</a>, signed under the gunboat diplomacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganghwa-island">Ganghwa Island</a> incident. Its agents assassinated Queen Min on 8 October 1895, and after its victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1894–1895 and over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1904–1905 it was left the sole regional power. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905">Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905</a> Korea became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> ruled through the Resident-General, and the Korean Army was cut from 20,000 to 1,000 men. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gojong-of-korea">Emperor Gojong</a> sent envoys to the Second Peace Conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1907 to question the protectorate, Japan forced him to relinquish his authority on 19 July 1907, and Sunjong became the last ruler of the Joseon dynasty.</p><p>The disbanded troops joined the "righteous armies", peasant militias that fought the Japanese until a scorched-earth campaign drove them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and Russia, and on 26 October 1909 Ahn Jung-geun assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Ito Hirobumi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harbin">Harbin</a>. On 22 August 1910 Ye Wanyong, Prime Minister of Korea, and Terauchi Masatake signed the Japan–Korea Treaty of 1910, by which the Emperor of Korea conceded his entire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the Emperor of Japan. Terauchi became the first Governor-General of Chōsen, and the years 1910–19 are known as the Military Police Reign Era.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Land Survey Bureau's cadastral surveys recognised ownership only on written proof, so cultivators with traditional rights lost their land, and Japanese individuals and companies such as the Oriental Development Company took over more and more of it. Tenants, all Korean, paid over half their crop in rent, and many left as labourers for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> was exported to Japan, per capita rice consumption among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> in 1932–1936 fell to half its level of 1912–1916. Japan built railways, ports and roads and, from the late 1920s under Governor-General Kazushige Ugaki, chemical plants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mills and munitions works. Economic output rose tenfold from 1910 to 1945, but in 1939 about 94 percent of factory capital was Japanese-owned, and scholars argue that much of the infrastructure was built to extract resources rather than to benefit Koreans.</p><p>In 1934 the Japanese in Chōsen numbered about 561,000 of over 21 million people, living largely in segregated districts of the cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> also became a major exporter of opium and narcotics in the 1930s, supplying the opium monopoly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> grew, from 86,000 Protestants and 79,000 Catholics in 1914 to 168,000 and 147,000 by 1934, and for some Koreans it became an expression of nationalism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> replaced the Korean police with its military police, the Kempeitai, under Akashi Motojiro, and combined the two forces in June 1910. Armed resistance continued from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and Primorsky Krai: Beom-do Hong's unit won the Battle of Bongodong in June 1920, and forces under Kim Chwajin and Hong killed about 1,500 Japanese soldiers at the Battle of Cheongsanri, after which the Imperial Japanese Army committed the Gando massacre, killing between 5,000 and tens of thousands of Korean civilians. The invasion of Manchuria in 1932 deprived many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups of their bases; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung">Kim Il Sung</a> was among the leaders there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-liberation-army">Korean Liberation Army</a>, founded by Kim Ku in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><p>Until 1944 enlistment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> in the Imperial Japanese Army was voluntary, though historians such as Pyo Young-Soo find systematic coercion behind it; from 1944 Korean men were conscripted into the army or the war industries, and about 200,000 were inducted. Of the 5,400,000 Koreans conscripted for labour, about 670,000 were taken to Japan, where 60,000 died; estimates of deaths in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and Manchuria range between 270,000 and 810,000. After the war 148 Koreans were convicted of Class B and C war crimes, many of them prison guards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was ruled by the Governor-General of Chōsen in Keijō, through 13 provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> were nominally granted Japanese citizenship but denied freedom of speech, assembly and an independent press; the Newspaper Act of 1907 and later closures left only government-backed papers until the cultural rule policy after 1919 allowed The Chosun Ilbo and The Dong-A Ilbo, and in 1940 all Korean language newspapers were shut again. The Peace Preservation Law of 1925 banned communist literature throughout the empire.</p><p>Policy rested on Nissen dōsoron, the theory that Koreans and Japanese shared mythological ancestors, which justified assimilation without equality. Schools taught the formation of the "Imperial Citizen", and the Regulations for Private Schools of 1911 aimed to undermine Korean institutions thought to breed patriotism. In 1938 policy turned to Naisen ittai; in 1942 the authorities closed the Korean Language Society, and by 1943 all Korean language courses had been phased out. Under the Sōshi-kaimei decrees of 1939 Koreans who kept their names could be refused school places, government services and food rations. From 1937 Koreans were required to worship the Emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines; around 2000 Christians who refused were imprisoned and 50 died as martyrs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colonial era shaped the peninsula long after it. The railway network linking Pusan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> and the Chinese border tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s economy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and carried troops to the continent; most of the infrastructure built then was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> of 1950–1953. Japan carried out the first modern archaeological excavations in Korea, and the Governor-General's law of 1933 to preserve historical artifacts is the origin of the National Treasures of both South and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>. Tens of thousands of cultural artifacts were taken to Japan: the South Korean government counts 75,311 taken from Korea, 34,369 of them in Japan.</p><p>Groups of the Korean diaspora date from the period: the Zainichi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> descend from about 600,000 who remained in Japan, 43,000 ethnic Koreans were trapped on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>, and Koreans who had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in 1937. Officers who had served Japan, among them Park Chung Hee, went on to careers in post-colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. The period remains hotly contested: some conservative Japanese nationalists portray it positively, while scholars such as Mark Caprio argue that Japan's policies systematically excluded most Koreans from the institutions of modernity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1939 the war turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> into a reservoir of labour and soldiers, and the Korean Provisional Government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, under the presidency of Kim Ku, declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 10 December 1941. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-liberation-army">Korean Liberation Army</a> prepared to return to the peninsula in the Eagle Project, but the sudden end of the war came first. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atomic-bombings-of-hiroshima-and-nagasaki">atomic bombings</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, where many Korean labourers were among the victims, and the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>, Japan surrendered on 15 August 1945, ending 35 years of colonial rule; Japanese troops remained in the south until mid-September.</p><p>American forces under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-r-hodge">John R. Hodge</a> arrived on 8 September 1945, while the Soviet Army held the north, and the peninsula was split at the 38th parallel, which led to the foundation of both North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> in 1948. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army Military Government issued a Name Restoration Order on 23 October 1946, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines were destroyed shortly after liberation. South Korea and Japan had no functional diplomatic relations until the Treaty on Basic Relations of 1965, which declared the treaties of 1910 and before "already null and void".</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> under Japanese rule was the colony of Chōsen, governed from Keijō by Japanese Governors-General from 1910 to 1945. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> built railways, ports and industry chiefly to serve its own needs, suppressed the independence movement, sought to erase Korean language and identity, and in the war years mobilised millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> as labourers, soldiers and sex slaves.</em></p>

